An @from for a column the table has already fills nothing, even when the
table is rebuilt: the column keeps its values, NULLs too. Filling an
existing column is a task in `init!`; an @from fills only a new one, so a
later rebuild never runs it twice.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'Pea Soup', NULL), (2, 'Rye Bread', 'bread');

== after
-- @release 1
-- @from 1 column dish slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'))
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT, note TEXT) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT, note TEXT) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, name, slug
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "name", "slug") SELECT "id", "name", "slug" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"
migrated from release 0

== query
SELECT id, slug FROM dish ORDER BY id

